[size=5][b]Master Looter[/b][/size]
[i]Auto-loot for Crimson Desert 2.01.00 with an in-game menu.[/i]

Walk past it and it is in your bag: dropped items, herbs and flowers, ore and stone chunks, timber, insects, fish, small animals and animal carcasses. Each kind has its own switch. Every item it picks up is checked against a database of 6,813 items with classes and tags, and the game's own Take-or-Steal check decides what is off limits. Skinning a carcass is the exception: the game hands the yield over without the mod seeing what it is, so that one switch is all or nothing. Everything is set from a menu inside the game; there is no ini to edit.

[size=4][b]What it collects[/b][/size]
Works as Kliff, Damiane or Oongka. Kliff does not need to be in the party. Every other auto-loot mod centres on the player-tagged actor, which as Damiane or Oongka is a fixture a kilometre from you; this one finds the body you are steering by the gear hanging off it.

Fourteen switches under Looting, each on its own:
[list]
[*]Ground items, including what enemies drop.
[*]Animal carcasses, skinned once each. Human corpses drop ordinary loot instead.
[*]Plants: herb, flower and mushroom nodes, and the same lying loose.
[*]Crops: barley, sweet potatoes and the rest of the game's vegetables, fruit and grain, on the plant or lying loose. Their own switch since 1.4.0, because before that turning Plants off still left most of a field being harvested.
[*]Ore, stone and wood, as chunks on the ground and as nodes that yield them. Ore veins are broken for you through the game's own path: the mod fires the two events your pickaxe fires, the swing landing and then the break, so the node spills its contents and disappears exactly as it does when you swing at it. What falls out is picked up from the ground. A vein pays one or two ore depending on whether you are carrying a mining bonus buff, and it pays a hand swing the same way, so the mod is not costing you anything there.
[*]Insects: butterflies, beetles, dragonflies, bees, spiders, scorpions, snails and the rest of what the game files as insects.
[*]Fish, and whatever else you catch in the water: crabs, shrimp, squid, starfish, seahorses.
[*]Small animals: rats, squirrels, birds, lizards, frogs, salamanders.
[*]Water from a well. Turn the handle yourself and the mod lifts the water out of the bucket, leaving the bucket on the well and the handle in your hands. Off by default.
[*]Unidentified nodes, containers and furniture nodes, all off by default.
[/list]
Gather nodes are armed from a distance, so a bush fills its data without you standing on it. Ore is reached for as far out as the scan can see, because a vein takes seconds to answer where a bush takes a fraction of one. Each kind has its own range, and the scan pace is yours to set.

[size=4][b]What it leaves alone[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Anything the game would call stealing. The mod asks the same routine the game uses to decide between "Take" and "Steal", and walks past whatever would put a bounty on you. It works that question out from the player, so it is ready the moment you load in rather than waiting for the game to ask first. You can opt in under Looting, Ownership.
[*]Quest items, memory chips, puzzle and mechanism parts, artifacts, recipes and your own equipment. The Classes and Items tabs can lift most of that on purpose, per class or per item. None of it reaches what a carcass pays out when skinned, which is why that switch is all or nothing.
[*]Fast-travel artifacts, memory triggers, locked nodes, gear worn by others and the contents of storage stacks, always.
[/list]

[size=4][b]The menu[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]General: the auto-loot switch, a burst button, keys, scan pace and the filters (quest items, unsellable items, a copper value floor, unnamed items).
[*]Looting: the fourteen switches, ranges, node arming and the ownership opt-in.
[*]Classes: one-click groups such as Weapons and armor, Damaged gear, Food and drink, Materials, Books and papers or Treasure and keepsakes, above a table of every class with its item count.
[*]Tags: always or never per tag; never beats always, and both beat the class rule.
[*]Items: search any of the 6,813 items, set an override, and see the verdict the rules give it right now.
[*]Presets: every setting and every rule saved under a name and swapped in two clicks. Settings are backed up each time the game starts and the last twelve are kept, so an update with different defaults is one button to undo. Anything that overwrites or discards asks first.
[*]A notice when the bag is full, with the slot count, the moment it fills.
[*]Simplified and Traditional Chinese are built in, translated by dofo7777, with a button each under General, Language. Chinese, Japanese and Korean render properly now; they used to come out as boxes. Any other language is a text file: a template of all 297 lines is on the GitHub page, or write one from the Language section. Fill in your language, drop the file beside the plugin, and set the language. A partial translation leaves the rest in English, and a file beside the plugin overrides a built-in one.
[*]Nearby: the objects around you with distance, class and the rule that decided each one, nearest first and without your own equipment in the way.
[*]Status: every signature and hook, the event path, counters, recent loot and the log.
[/list]
Watch mode keeps the menu on screen while you play, so you can read the Nearby list or the log with the controls still yours.

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[b]With Definitive Mod Manager (DMM)[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Download the DMM file and import it (drag it onto the DMM window). DMM registers MasterLooter.asi as an ASI add-on.
[*]Remove or disable any other auto-loot mod. Two of them hook the same game functions and the second one to load does nothing.
[*]Enable it and Mount.
[/list]
[b]By hand[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Ultimate ASI Loader (winmm.dll) must be in the game's bin64 folder.
[*]Copy MasterLooter.asi into bin64 next to winmm.dll while the game is closed. The item database and the creature table are built into the plugin.
[*]Start the game and press Insert.
[/list]
Uninstall by deleting the MasterLooter.* files from bin64 (the plugin writes MasterLooter.ini and .log next to itself). No game file is modified and nothing is written to a save.

[size=4][b]Antivirus[/b][/size]
Three scanners out of seventy-one flag the plugin, and all three are machine-learning models: Microsoft (Trojan:Win32/Wacatac.B!ml), Symantec (ML.Attribute.HighConfidence) and Deep Instinct. Two say so on the label: !ml and ML.Attribute both mean a model guessed from the shape of the file. None matched anything known, and the sixty-eight that did not flag it read the same file as clean. The DMM archive scores one of sixty-seven.

Worth knowing that the number moves on its own. 1.2.1 scored four, 1.4.0 four, 1.5.0 two, 1.5.1 four, 1.6.0 two, 1.6.1 three and 1.6.2 three. Microsoft and Symantec have flagged every one. CrowdStrike Falcon and Deep Instinct come and go, and between the last two releases they swapped places. Nothing about what the file does changed in between. That is a model guessing, as against a scanner recognising something.

Both 1.6.2 reports are public: [url=https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/660326ae232d840634f33c4961cc7b49dfbff44cdbead4b9000ffd9eb3e6279d]the plugin[/url] and [url=https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/5a1c883c8d7b188c049318b966d2febbb04ad9092af5f697542de62537abc08f]the package[/url].

The shape is the whole reason. It is an unsigned DLL that a loader puts inside the game, and once there it patches instructions in memory, searches the game's code for byte patterns, reads the keyboard before the game does and draws over Direct3D. A trainer does all of that, so a model trained on trainers answers trainer. It carries no signing certificate and has no install history behind it yet to argue otherwise.

What it does not do is reach the network. It imports no networking library at all, writes MasterLooter.ini, .log, .presets and .backups beside itself and nothing else, reads and writes no registry key, and modifies no game file. Every line is on GitHub and one batch file builds it, if you would rather compile your own. If your browser or Defender blocks the download, restore the file and exclude the game's bin64 folder.

SHA-256 for 1.6.2:
[code]5a1c883c8d7b188c049318b966d2febbb04ad9092af5f697542de62537abc08f  MasterLooter-1.6.2-DMM.zip
ae2b11083b6e9f0fd99997dd4b5a9bb01ff69a63f1bb75b123cdeff4cea723f2  MasterLooter-1.6.2.zip
660326ae232d840634f33c4961cc7b49dfbff44cdbead4b9000ffd9eb3e6279d  MasterLooter.asi[/code]

[size=4][b]Controls[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Insert opens and closes the menu. Escape also closes it.
[*]F10 turns auto-loot on and off. F11 loots everything in range once.
[*]Home is watch mode: the menu stays up while the game keeps every input. Insert makes it interactive again.
[*]While the menu is interactive the game does not see keyboard, mouse or controller; key releases still pass so nothing sticks.
[/list]
All keys are rebindable under General. They also sit in MasterLooter.ini beside the plugin as MenuKey, KeyToggle, KeyBurst and KeyWatch, written as virtual-key codes; edit the file and save it and the change is picked up about a second later without a restart, which is the quickest way to clear a clash with another ASI mod. Each of the four also takes a controller shortcut of two buttons at once, never one, because every single button already does something in this game. The mod only reads the pad, so the game still sees both buttons: pick a pair that does nothing together, such as the two shoulder buttons, or Back and a face button. Shortcuts are ignored while the game window is not in front.

If the menu opens but will not take a click, the cursor is the usual cause and not the menu. In borderless windowed mode the Windows pointer and the game's own pointer drift apart, so clicks land somewhere other than where you are pointing. Move the pointer to the top left corner once and the two snap back together. Thanks to LuxDragon for working that one out.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Built for Crimson Desert 2.01.00 (exe 1.0.0.2760). Every game address comes from a byte pattern or a class name resolved at load, so a small update does not necessarily break it; the Status tab shows what resolved and what did not, and the log says the same in more detail.
[*]Works with DLSS frame generation and HDR. The menu is drawn before frame generation and composited for the swap chain's colour space.
[*]Keyboard, mouse and controller are all handled while the menu is up.
[*]Requires Ultimate ASI Loader (winmm.dll) in bin64. DMM installs it for you.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Safety and known limits[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]The mod sends the game the same events it sends itself when you press the interaction key, and reads memory only to decide what to send.
[*]A carcass is searched once per session and never again, whatever the retry setting: searching an empty carcass has been seen to duplicate items, and duplicates are how saves get corrupted.
[*]No line-of-sight check. Ranges are distances, not visibility. Keep the arming range short or a node behind a thin wall can be gathered.
[*]Creatures the mod cannot name are only caught when every category they could belong to is on. Bugs are recognised by their model, fish by theirs; a few unusual creatures may stay unidentified and be left alone.
[*]Gather nodes are identified by the prefab they were placed from, matched against a table of 1,076 node kinds built from the game's own data. A node outside that table is identified by what lands in your bag when it is gathered, and is otherwise left alone until you turn Unidentified nodes on.
[*]Chests and storage boxes open a window rather than hand over an item; they are off by default.
[*]Ownership is answered by the game's own routine, not by a guess here. If the mod ever cannot get an answer it refuses the object and says so rather than risking a bounty.
[*]Single player. Do not use it anywhere it would give you an advantage over other people.
[/list]

[size=4][b]If something is wrong[/b][/size]
MasterLooter.log sits in the game's bin64 folder beside the plugin, which on a Steam install means ...\steamapps\common\Crimson Desert\bin64\MasterLooter.log. The Status tab prints the full path and has a button that opens the folder. It is written from the moment the plugin loads, so a crash before anything is drawn still leaves one, and it is rewritten every launch. It lists which signatures resolved, and for every object within range that was skipped, once, the rule that skipped it. Post the relevant lines with a report: what was taken or left, where you were, and what you expected. A report is useful in both directions: something taken that should have stayed, or something left that should have been taken.

